Nary, 40, has turned her life around and those of her children after taking part in positive parenting training sessions with her husband Ben Sreng.
Every morning she cycles 6km from her home to the riverside next to the Royal Palace where she makes a living selling seeds to tourists who feed the birds near the Palace grounds. Nary was an orphan and cannot even remember her parents’ faces.
Both work seven days a week, from dawn until dusk. In the past, their hardship sometimes overwhelmed them and caused them to be violent towards their children.
Every day I felt heavy like a stone was inside my body. I asked why my children were so disobedient and why they could not be like other children.
“My children did not listen to us. Also, we did not listen to them.”
Although a family is the primary safety net for children, many parents and caregivers suffering hardships believe their children are better off in residential care, unaware of the risks and have little knowledge of more suitable alternatives. This often results in unnecessary family separation.
This is a method of child-raising based on mutual respect that provides children with safe, stable and nurturing environments so they can develop into healthy, respectful and productive adults.
This strategy serves as a guide for professionals working with children and families so they can provide more coordinated and quality support for parents and caregivers.
Sen Nary and her husband, Ben Sreng
Through this activity, Nary and Sreng have learnt about different forms of violence; its negative impacts; and the risks of not providing adequate care and protection for children.
“When I attended the training and the trainer talked about the negative impacts of violence and unnecessary family separation, I cried because I recalled being a child without parents. I had to live with others and I was abused by them,” she said.
“I have started using sweet words with my children. My children now listen to me better,” Nary said.
This demonstrates that violence against children is not only fundamentally wrong – it is also harmful to a country’s social and economic progress.
Positive discipline is a method of teaching appropriate behaviour by interacting with children in a kind but firm manner. It reinforces children’s rights to protection from violence and uses discipline that respects their dignity.
Why do we need positive discipline?
When children were asked about incidents of violence they experienced, both, female and male students said they felt “embarrassed”, “sad”, “angry” and “scared”. Some even stated they no longer wanted to attend school.
To put an end to violence in schools, five-day pilot trainings as part of the positive discipline programme were carried out in Kampot, Prey Veng, and Battambang in 2015. Based on the success of this pilot, the programme has continued to expand to additional districts across these provinces.
Siem Reap Province, Cambodia, September 2017 – Mok Pen commune is located in rural Siem Reap, where the resorts and attractions of the provincial capital are replaced by rice fields.
There is a small courtyard outside the house littered with rubbish where chickens and stray dogs roam.
With no source of income in their home village, Bopha and Sopheak’s parents had to leave their family behind two years ago in search of work, as did 53,000 other Siem Reap residents who were documented as leaving the province in 2014.
In two years of working away, the parents have only been able to return home once for the Khmer New Year in April, 2017. They were only able to give their family a small amount of cash during this trip back home.
