The term ICO stands for Initial Coin Offering.
Investors in an ICO are making an acquisition of a token early which is different from an IPO. The respective token will have a precise utility within a specific platform that is developed by the representatives of the ICO.
In order to dodge the respective legal regulations to the investment market, the developers of the project defend at all costs that the acquisition of tokens in this phase is not exactly an investment. The investor gets the chance to make an acquisition of the token.
The lack of governance in the ICO market is complicated, and this has caused many investors to lose their money. Several unfinished projects have been previously published as white papers, and this has led to an added fear for the investors.
This will actively allow token holders to make decisions about the future of the company. Several decentralized autonomous organizations that are formed after an ICO allow the users of the acquired tokens to determine in which elements of the money that has been collected during the ICO itself will be spent.
