Thermal expansion and contraction are the inherent characteristics of materials, and changes in the temperature difference of the environment lead to changes in the size of the materials, which are the main factors that damage the external insulation system. When temperature changes and rain or freezing work together, its destructive is greater.
Plastering mortar (or anti-cracking mortar) is an important component of the external insulation system. Highly flexible mortar can improve the anti-cracking ability of the mortar, thereby preventing the infiltration of water.
At present, there are two kinds of reinforced mesh cloths that are widely used in external insulation systems: alkali-resistant glass fiber mesh cloth and steel wire mesh. Enhancing the use of mesh cloth can effectively improve the tensile strength of the protective layer and prevent the system from cracking.
The external thermal insulation system using facing bricks has certain requirements for the moisture content, size and unit area quality of facing bricks. Due to the great damage to the external thermal insulation system by water penetration, we have higher requirements for the waterproof and flexible properties of the jointing materials than before.
